# The national unit for britain. Items take n=0; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g6-britain-how-our-country-is-governed v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=9 subject=history nation=britain levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "How the United Kingdom is governed"
  goal "Put the steps by which Britain came to be governed by an elected Parliament in order, and say what Parliament does."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1200 to=2000 step=100 years=[1215, 1265, 1918] labels=["Magna Carta", "De Montfort's Parliament", "Women vote in Britain"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      In 1215 King John sealed Magna Carta, which said that the king too must keep the law. In 1265 Simon de Montfort called a parliament with men from the towns as well as lords. Over the centuries Parliament came to make the laws. Women first voted in 1918, and since 1969 everyone aged 18 can vote for their Member of Parliament.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n6-what n=0
    }
    show history.n6-order n=0 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n6-between n=0 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n6-century n=0 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.law-steps k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.vote-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.un-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n6-order n=0 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n6-what n=0
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n6-century n=0 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.law-steps k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n6-between n=0 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n6-why n=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n6-find-out n=0
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is how the family's own country is governed today and how it came to be, told as
    facts and without verdicts. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews how laws are made, votes and
    the United Nations; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. England's citizenship programme at key stage 3 teaches the development of democratic government in the United Kingdom (recalled, not quoted from a fetched page); this lesson is a first step.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
